#!/bin/sh
# script/job-mix: rehearse the multi-tasking workload sweep under QEMU (milestone 168).
#
#   script/job-mix                          # aarch64
#   script/job-mix --arch riscv64           # the architecture radon runs
#   script/job-mix --arch riscv64 --smp 4   # radon's core count
#   script/job-mix --release                # the optimisation level script/board-image builds
#   script/job-mix --hvf --release --smp 4  # the Apple cores (aarch64); refused until milestone 227
#
# `design/decisions/96-process-kernel-or-event-kernel.md` asks whether this kernel should keep a
# kernel stack per thread. Three of its four inputs are settled; the fourth is performance, and the
# retrospective it rests on found event kernels within 1% of process kernels on micro-benchmarks and
# 20% ahead on a multi-tasking workload. Every instrument this project owns is a micro-benchmark.
# This is the one that is not: a pool of EL0 tasks running a heterogeneous job mix, swept over task
# count, reported as jobs per minute. See crates/job_mix for the mix and notes/job-mix.md for what a
# result means.
#
# **This is the rehearsal, not the measurement.** The milestone's gate is HARDWARE, and it means it:
# under TCG the magnitudes are fiction (no cache is modelled) and under HVF a host scheduler sits
# under every guest thread. What this proves is that the workload runs, that the sweep completes,
# and that the output is the shape a bench evening will read. The number is taken on radon, by
# notes/job-mix.md's procedure.
#
# What it boots is `--features job_mix`, where the boot tour ends in the sweep instead of a halt.
# See kernel/src/job_mix.rs and fixtures/src/job_mix_task.rs.
#
# Exit statuses:
#
#   0  the sweep completed and printed every point
#   1  the kernel refused to start the sweep (`job-mix: FAILED`, printed above)
#   3  QEMU ended before the sweep finished
#   4  the build failed, or the arguments were wrong
#
# BUGS
#
# - **A jobs-per-minute figure from one boot is a draw, not a result.** notes/soak.md records four
#   runs on radon whose rates span fifteenfold, and milestone 240's census explains them: the
#   boot-time thread-placement lottery decides. The kernel prints its census before the sweep for
#   exactly that reason, and notes/job-mix.md's procedure asks for repeated boots.
# - **There is no `--check` and no committed baseline.** `script/bench` gates against
#   bench/baseline-*.txt because its icount counts are deterministic; a sweep whose whole subject is
#   scheduling under contention is not, on any accelerator this tree has. Gating it would be
#   asserting a tolerance nobody has measured.
# - **Nothing builds `--features job_mix` unless somebody runs this script, and it had already
#   rotted.** `script/lint`'s per-feature clippy loop carries `shell`, `bench`, `icount`, `board`,
#   `fastpath_pad`, `soak_test` and `cycle_counter_grant`, and not this one; milestone 296 found the
#   feature failing `-D warnings` on both ISAs (`user::boot_via_progenitor` dead, because a job-mix
#   boot replaces the handoff the way a soak boot does and the `allow` named only `soak`) and fixed
#   that one line. Nothing keeps it fixed. Not added to the loop here: that is two more clippy
#   builds on every pull request, and the shape of the answer is
#   `design/roadmap/proposals/board-only-features-nothing-compiles.md`, which covers `reboot_soak_test`
#   and `single_hart` too. **This entry said "CI builds neither this feature nor `--features soak_test`"
#   until 2026-09-14 and the second half was false**: `soak` has been in that loop since it was
#   written. A BUGS section that overstates is the failure mode AGENTS.md names, so it is corrected
#   rather than quietly narrowed.
# - **`--hvf` does not run on this project's QEMU (11.1.1) today.** QEMU refuses HVF with a GICv2
#   and the kernel drives only GICv2, so the run exits 3 with QEMU's own message. Milestone 227 (a
#   GICv3 driver) is the fix; notes/job-mix.md's cross-check section has the 2026-09-19 attempt.
# - **The output changed on 2026-09-19**: each point is the median of 21 repeats, printed as
#   `ticks_median=` and `jpm_median=` beside the ends, and a `job-mix-kind:` line per job kind
#   follows each point. A transcript from before then says `ticks=` and `jpm=`, is the best of 3 of
#   a five-job mix, and is not comparable. notes/job-mix.md has the table.
# - **This sweep and `script/soak-test` are alternatives, not a stack.** Both replace the end of the boot
#   tour, and a build carrying both features would run this one and never soak.
# - **No result from this instrument is comparable with `script/bench`'s.** The mix, the task count
#   and the metric are all different, and the jobs are not the primitives.
#
# Name: provisional, this lane's coinage (2026-09-04, milestone 168). Hyphenated because script/
# entry points are (AGENTS.md's naming table), and named for the workload rather than for the
# machine it is aimed at. `aim` and `aim7` were refused for claiming somebody else's benchmark:
# this keeps AIM7's four methodological properties and none of its 53 jobs, and crates/job_mix's
# BUGS is explicit that the two are not comparable. `multitasking` was refused as an adjective where
# the tenet asks for a noun.

set -e
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

# This project's own QEMU, if `script/ci-qemu` has built one (scripts/qemu-path.sh says why
# PATH is the mechanism). A no-op on macOS and on any machine without that prefix.
. scripts/qemu-path.sh

exec cargo xtask job-mix "$@"
